1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,440
Alessandro, Brett, Matthew, it's fantastic to have this opportunity to speak with all of you today.

2
00:00:06,480 --> 00:00:08,160
So, Brett, I want to dive right in.

3
00:00:09,680 --> 00:00:16,800
What are the main challenges that you think people need to be aware of regarding emerging technology and the way it's going to impact society?

4
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:22,080
Yeah, so I think there's some surface level challenges that a lot of people are already noticing.

5
00:00:23,520 --> 00:00:32,960
And in particular, I think with any new technology, there's this tendency to outsource certain human functions and faculties that the technology makes easier.

6
00:00:33,040 --> 00:00:46,240
So I think with AI in particular, you're looking at a big shift in education and the way that we come to know things and know the world, which is very different than what we experienced with text and some previous media.

7
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:50,480
So, you know, I think there's a almost an epistemic shift happening.

8
00:00:50,800 --> 00:00:53,120
So how we perceive, how we know.

9
00:00:53,440 --> 00:01:00,960
And so as an educator, I think about that because knowing and learning is a process of adequating the intellect to reality.

10
00:01:01,920 --> 00:01:08,640
And the more virtual that our mediums become for accessing that reality, that adequation becomes.

11
00:01:09,440 --> 00:01:10,400
a little more removed.

12
00:01:10,960 --> 00:01:17,440
And so I'm also a parent and with children, you know, so much of learning is somatic, it's, you know, social.

13
00:01:18,640 --> 00:01:28,720
And so these are elements, I think, that technology tempts us to replace or modify in ways that aren't always necessarily good for human development.

14
00:01:29,360 --> 00:01:41,040
And Brett, if you look at technology's ability to make certain aspects of, you know, of accessing information, of sharing information, of kind of democratizing that information, that all seems fantastic.

15
00:01:41,600 --> 00:01:42,640
Where do you feel that?

16
00:01:43,760 --> 00:01:52,320
critical aspects of that pursuit of knowledge or of the hard work of getting knowledge, is that what you're saying is going to, you know, we're going to load or we're not going to have that experience?

17
00:01:53,280 --> 00:01:53,760
Yeah, yeah.

18
00:01:53,920 --> 00:02:04,000
Well, so I think there's some knowledge we gain through the sort of synthetic search of a database of information that can reveal new things to us about the world.

19
00:02:04,880 --> 00:02:08,080
But so much of learning happens in an embodied way, in a social way.

20
00:02:09,120 --> 00:02:20,320
And so I think for developers, for educators, for parents, for policymakers, I think the questions we have to wrestle with are those sort of ripple effects of the new technology.

21
00:02:20,640 --> 00:02:22,000
What are the epistemic effects?

22
00:02:22,160 --> 00:02:26,400
How do we come to know the world differently through these mediums than we did before?

23
00:02:26,800 --> 00:02:39,680
So yes, we have this sort of torrent of information that's available to us through these networks and databases, but then we get into the problem of discernment and sifting of what is real and what is false.

24
00:02:40,800 --> 00:02:50,160
That requires, I think, a second level of intellectual capability that, yeah, again, a teacher, a parent has to be there for.

25
00:02:50,800 --> 00:03:00,320
And so the idea that we can, again, load some of these functions, education and so on without the sort of human intermediary is risky.

26
00:03:01,520 --> 00:03:07,360
And Alexandra, what are some of the challenges that you see with emerging technology that you're that you're worried about or you're thinking about?

27
00:03:08,320 --> 00:03:24,800
I think one is just the personal formation of the person, what it means to live and seek for a life of virtue and some of the obstacles that these newer technologies are presenting as kind of shifting our gaze away from what is our ultimate search for virtue and rights.

28
00:03:26,400 --> 00:03:54,800
I think when we are in an environment where more and more becomes easy and we try and think about how we can be most efficient to do the least amount of work to achieve comfort, I think we avoid the reality that labor is inherent in man and it's in our labor, in our work that we discover our call, that we apply our gifts as stewards, not as gods in and of ourselves.

29
00:03:55,360 --> 00:03:58,240
And I think we see that more in the educational setting when it comes to.

30
00:03:58,320 --> 00:04:07,200
to students writing papers, for example, like part of what it means to develop the intellect and your soul and understanding of the good is to wrestle with the topics.

31
00:04:08,320 --> 00:04:15,920
In some ways, the answer is not the essay or the answers to the test, but you're wrestling to understand and implement those in your lives.

32
00:04:16,320 --> 00:04:21,200
I think that extends to our work as individuals, whether it's knowledge work or physical work.

33
00:04:23,440 --> 00:04:32,320
The more we kind of outsource the thinking, the wrestling with reality, the more we go into the virtual world, where there isn't the physical suffering and hard labor.

34
00:04:32,320 --> 00:05:04,560
hard labour and we retreat into the purely mental act of deliberation, I think we lose part of what it means to be incarnate beings and that can really obscure, I think, the reality of man and that has many social and personal consequences, I think that's contributing a lot to the anxiety, the isolation issues that we see, the further deterioration of community and all its social effects, but ultimately also the theological and spiritual community, which is not great.

35
00:05:05,280 --> 00:05:06,400
And Matthew, what, what about you?

36
00:05:07,520 --> 00:05:13,760
What challenges are you thinking about when you see the way emerging technology is entering ever more presently into our day to day life?

37
00:05:14,560 --> 00:05:24,800
I think if I had to pick one thing, the thing that kind of concerns me the most is not so much the technology, it's the fact that we know that the technology is going to be more and more, right?

38
00:05:25,120 --> 00:05:36,160
And in large part because maybe there's a spiritual poverty there where people just don't, you know, especially with AI and robots doing more and more in society, there'll be, I think, more and more people with a lot of time on their hands.

39
00:05:36,960 --> 00:05:40,400
My concern is what people will do constructively with that time, right?

40
00:05:42,080 --> 00:05:50,000
If they don't know the value of developing one's spiritual life or developing friendships, right?

41
00:05:50,320 --> 00:06:02,320
Or finding a spouse, or they don't know how to even begin that journey, then I worry that a lot of people will just, without even really like wrestling with it, just kind of opt to disappear into gaming worlds or virtual reality.

42
00:06:03,600 --> 00:06:10,240
And what kind of concerns me and I feel very convicted about is I feel like a lot of people are going to start, you know, undertake that journey.

43
00:06:10,640 --> 00:06:15,600
And of course, you know, they'll be jagging those dopaminees off a cliff because ultimately they're not going to be satisfying, right?

44
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:20,800
And that existential cliff, as some call it, is something that concerns me the most.

45
00:06:21,440 --> 00:06:28,960
That is that a lot of people will simply, well, will use these technologies, will chase those dopamine hits and arrive at the edge of that cliff.

46
00:06:29,680 --> 00:06:33,600
And because they have no other hope, they don't know of any other way, they'll just, they'll leap, right?

47
00:06:33,840 --> 00:06:42,080
And so I think the thing that concerns me the most is ensuring that the church recognizes that we have to be far better at leveraging this technology in as Catholic a way as possible.

48
00:06:42,320 --> 00:06:47,920
If for no other reason, we have an obligation to ensure that the truth and our Christian way of life is made present to as many people as possible.

49
00:06:48,800 --> 00:06:56,240
And Brad, if I may turn to you, how different in terms of a challenge for society is AI to past technology?

50
00:06:56,560 --> 00:07:58,960
I know, you know, growing up when I was in high school, social media, and more particularly in college, social media really started to start to become something that was part of everyday life, it was almost moment to moment, and people called like kind of 2010 to 2020, really that social media era changed our, you know, the way we run political campaigns, it changed marketing, and it's obviously, you know, had some aspects that are very fantastic that people are able to communicate and stay in touch, but there's been an enormous emotional mental cost to that and it's really you know it's inserted itself into everything how different is AI to past challenges that we as a society have had to confront yeah I think it feels very different although I used to think of these technology epochs as being kind of almost hermetically sealed right we went from oral to scribal to print and somehow you know when we went from scribal to print all the manuscripts just disappeared up right and that wasn't the case there was this whole period of transition between the two media that really defined how the new medium got adopted and used.

51
00:07:59,120 --> 00:08:06,080
And I think we're in that transitional moment right now between, as you say, social media, but I would also include television.

52
00:08:07,040 --> 00:08:26,960
And so, you know, when you come out of a paradigm like television and social media that's so driven by advertising, entertainment, fantasy, connection, then naturally the new medium is going to kind of import those sensibilities and try to kind of reverse them or resell them in a new skin.

53
00:08:27,200 --> 00:08:30,560
And so I think we're just now starting to see what a...

54
00:08:30,560 --> 00:08:35,760
what AI is capable of that previous media were not, and that's the exciting part.

55
00:08:36,080 --> 00:08:53,920
I think in some ways it's the unsexy stuff of like being able to read massive amounts of medical telemetry data to make better diagnoses or not that it's unsexy, Matthew, but to catalog libraries, right, of sources that many people didn't have access to before.

56
00:08:54,160 --> 00:08:55,680
So a big boon for researchers.

57
00:08:56,320 --> 00:09:01,520
But again, if you think about where we came from with television, social media, people want to be entertained.

58
00:09:01,760 --> 00:09:02,560
They want to be amused.

59
00:09:02,720 --> 00:09:03,600
They want to be distracted.

60
00:09:04,160 --> 00:09:16,000
And so chatbots and generative AI and these kind of virtual imaginary spaces become very profitable, but they're not always maybe the best use of the technology.

61
00:09:16,160 --> 00:09:19,280
So I think that's the old, right, that sense.

62
00:09:19,520 --> 00:09:32,080
And I think the newer things that are happening are the more exciting and maybe we're more mature now in our technology use that we might be able to move beyond just sort of creating new fantasies with every new technology that we develop.

63
00:09:32,800 --> 00:09:48,000
And Alessandra, when you see new things that AI is able to provide and bring to the world, what excites you, where do you see that there's the most opportunity or the most ability to kind of, I guess, create that positive impact that we're looking to do?

64
00:09:49,040 --> 00:09:52,080
Yeah, maybe one quick comment on the prior question.

65
00:09:52,320 --> 00:10:00,720
I think there are ways in which it's a progression down the same type of spectrum, and I think there are ways in which it's creating a new fundamental lens to approach the world.

66
00:10:01,040 --> 00:10:16,800
I think there's a way of viewing the last few hundred years of the first industrial evolution being about trying to incorporate as much human capital into productive, concentrated use and the application of human labor and capital producing basically the middle class, right?

67
00:10:16,960 --> 00:10:55,920
The industrial middle class in the capitalist context in the initial advent of the software revolution that started to change by trying to add the concept of operating leverage to business and it's how do we have a given amount of capital have the highest leverage by minimizing human labor per unit of capital that created massively transformative companies transformed how we interact with the world but also furthered continued concentration of wealth and hollowed out in many ways the middle class, the industrial middle class and the economy in the in the US I think in some ways AI is the like approaching at the limit of that where you need billion dollar companies made by one person.

68
00:10:56,320 --> 00:10:56,480
Right.

69
00:10:56,560 --> 00:10:59,520
And so that's in a way progression down that pathway.

70
00:11:00,640 --> 00:11:17,920
But in a different way, I don't think we confused any of the mediums that Brett talked about that we interacted with TV, social media as intelligence and kind of the introduction of this idea of like we are now approaching what humans are just in it with a different substrate.

71
00:11:18,720 --> 00:11:43,280
And I think a kind of pernicious assumption there that sneaks in is that all humans are is just really complex calculation in a way that LLMs manifest as an example manifest in interactive language but are inherently matrix math to be overly simplistic that gives us a really, I think, malformed idea of what a human is.

72
00:11:43,920 --> 00:11:58,000
And I think that can creep in in really dangerous ways on how we look at ourselves and trying a maximization of the cans of our life instead of the search of the odds of what we're being drawn to on a spiritual level.

73
00:11:58,320 --> 00:12:00,800
So just some comment on that.

74
00:12:01,360 --> 00:12:02,400
Alessandro, I love that.

75
00:12:02,480 --> 00:12:03,840
And if I can just jump on that.

76
00:12:04,320 --> 00:12:21,040
One thing that we're seeing very clearly is that a lot of the people who are driving these major AI companies, these major tech companies, they have an ideology, a philosophical outlook that is very, you know, there's very many of them are transhuman and they see that people are exactly that, these calculators.

77
00:12:22,880 --> 00:12:24,320
First of all, I have two questions.

78
00:12:25,520 --> 00:12:31,440
What does that do when the drivers, the leaders in these industries are pushing that type of philosophical and ideological outlook.

79
00:12:31,600 --> 00:12:32,640
How does that change our society?

80
00:12:32,960 --> 00:12:34,320
And of course, how can we push back?

81
00:12:35,360 --> 00:12:45,920
And secondly, if there isn't a resistance that's proper, that's able to push back on that at a certain point, this is kind of what Matthew was talking about with the existential cliff.

82
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:46,480
People can lose hope.

83
00:12:46,640 --> 00:12:51,760
And that's, of course, as Catholics, we have a duty to be people of hope and the church has a duty to respond.

84
00:12:52,240 --> 00:12:53,440
Brett, if I can throw that to you.

85
00:12:54,080 --> 00:12:54,160
Yeah.

86
00:12:56,160 --> 00:13:00,960
The trans thing is really pernicious in a way because it is insidious.

87
00:13:01,040 --> 00:13:02,400
It's kind of baked in technology.

88
00:13:03,760 --> 00:13:14,480
is always, I think, ideological and political, and it's not always clear in the application or the surface use of it that there's something going on behind the scenes that's deeply philosophical.

89
00:13:16,160 --> 00:13:20,640
I mean, in some ways I feel like we're retrieving the Middle Ages in a way.

90
00:13:20,800 --> 00:13:22,160
We're trying to do alchemy again.

91
00:13:22,240 --> 00:13:23,840
We're trying to turn math into language.

92
00:13:24,640 --> 00:13:31,840
It's almost kind of kabbalistic kind of conjuring language out of these mathematical, statistical tokens and so on.

93
00:13:32,160 --> 00:13:38,960
So I think the idea that we can reduce humans or human intelligence and reason to that is troubling.

94
00:13:39,200 --> 00:13:47,840
And of course, we've all heard the sort of Ray Kurzweil mythologies about the singularity and offloading and uploading consciousness to machines and so on.

95
00:13:49,040 --> 00:14:01,040
It feels like we're getting closer to that in a way, but we're also not defining consciousness in the right way as something that's part of a soul that has faculties of memory and imagination and will and so on.

96
00:14:01,200 --> 00:14:09,680
So I think recovering to your point about pushing back and the church's way of doing that is to retrieve a human or a church's understanding of the soul.

97
00:14:11,200 --> 00:14:18,960
in all of its complexity and not its sort of reduced post-enlightened just kind of reason and thought function.

98
00:14:20,720 --> 00:14:35,040
If I could, if wisdom is not a virtue that the soul develops, if it's just being really good at logic or math, then the natural result is transhumanism because humans are not great calculators relative to bigger and bigger GPU clusters.

99
00:14:35,520 --> 00:14:47,360
And as a second order effect of that, even within humans, then you start to look at people who are, let's say, on a relative to the best human, less equipped with that type of intelligence as being inherently lesser than.

100
00:14:47,760 --> 00:14:54,320
And you have a, I mean, there's profound implications there of how we treat the poor, right, which is in direct opposition to our social teaching.

101
00:14:54,800 --> 00:15:05,200
They become not those with a preferential option, but those that are unnecessary and or obsolete within humanity, much less humanity in general, relative to machines.

102
00:15:05,440 --> 00:15:08,000
So, I mean, that is the natural progression of that.

103
00:15:08,480 --> 00:15:09,680
It's also a new gnosticism.

104
00:15:09,760 --> 00:15:09,840
Yeah.

105
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:13,360
I mean, it's an abdication and escape from the body.

106
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:20,240
I mean, if you read the sort of gnostic lore, and this is what we're doing again it again in a technological guise.

107
00:15:20,640 --> 00:15:32,560
And I think, you know, the church has this long history of combating heresies and understanding these perversions and distortions of what it means to be human and what it means to have a soul and what our relationship is to Christ and to God.

108
00:15:33,120 --> 00:15:41,360
And so I think retrieving those sources, which is what I think AI and the internet in particular is good at, is it is a memory machine.

109
00:15:41,600 --> 00:15:46,240
Computers are great at storing a lot of information and giving us access to that information.

110
00:15:46,960 --> 00:15:53,440
I mentioned television earlier, which I think in some ways as an age wasn't great because it was about projecting fantasies.

111
00:15:53,680 --> 00:16:00,640
There was a world without memory that just kept manufacturing new stories, new fantasies to tell itself about reality.

112
00:16:00,800 --> 00:16:05,680
And now we're actually going back to memory and what we remember as a culture.

113
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:07,120
So that's a good thing.

114
00:16:07,680 --> 00:16:19,440
I think it's important to acknowledge that a lot of the problems that we're being confronted with are the result of a vacuum, which in large part the church and all of us are kind of responsible for, right?

115
00:16:19,680 --> 00:16:23,040
I mean, we haven't been as successful in evangelization as we should be.

116
00:16:23,360 --> 00:16:25,280
And in turn, a vacuum has been created.

117
00:16:26,480 --> 00:16:29,600
You know, the devil is filling all kinds of crazy ideas.

118
00:16:29,840 --> 00:16:38,000
And so like one of the challenges is going to be for us, I think, like for the church is, is addressing this kind of, um, the poverty of teleology, right?

119
00:16:38,400 --> 00:16:42,080
This, this forgetting what human life is so speech-tricky about.

120
00:16:42,320 --> 00:16:53,840
Like when I think about, like, for instance, Elon Musk with Neuralink, and I, and I'm, I'm a big fan of Elon Musk in many ways, so I'm not knocking on him, but I do worry, I mean, Neuralink is amazing in the sense that right now it's, it's helping people, it's restoring people's full humanity, right?

121
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:57,520
Right now they're helping paraplegics and things like that to move and interact with computer interface.

122
00:16:57,600 --> 00:16:58,720
That's all, that's all cool, right?

123
00:16:58,880 --> 00:17:00,560
And I think as insofar as technology is.

124
00:17:01,040 --> 00:17:03,920
restoring people to their full humanity, we should be looking to integrate and use it.

125
00:17:04,480 --> 00:17:07,120
What worries me is the next phase of this technology, right?

126
00:17:07,360 --> 00:17:12,320
Where we're integrating the technology into our brains to expand our computational capabilities.

127
00:17:12,960 --> 00:17:15,280
And I think the big question is why are we doing this, right?

128
00:17:15,520 --> 00:17:18,800
And I think for some, it comes from this kind of utilitarian perspective.

129
00:17:18,960 --> 00:17:26,800
Well, machines are getting smart and this is what Alessandro was saying, computers are getting better and better at computation, which means they're going to be able to deliver more and more value to society.

130
00:17:27,200 --> 00:17:30,160
Well, I don't want humans to like not being valuable in this domain.

131
00:17:30,400 --> 00:17:32,640
And so we have to find a way to make sure humans stay relevant.

132
00:17:32,720 --> 00:17:37,840
So we have to find ways to expand our computational capabilities so that we can interact with machines more effectively and add value, right?

133
00:17:38,880 --> 00:17:43,200
And I can understand if your TLT ideology is something like getting to Mars, right?

134
00:17:43,360 --> 00:17:46,160
That's what your whole life is about, is getting humanity to Mars.

135
00:17:46,640 --> 00:17:53,680
And I can see why, if that's what life is all about for you, why you'd want to expand your computational capabilities so that you can actually be actively part of that project.

136
00:17:54,160 --> 00:17:57,040
But if your TLT ideology is about becoming a saint, right?

137
00:17:57,280 --> 00:18:01,120
And about building the kingdom of heaven, then that shouldn't be a priority, right?

138
00:18:01,360 --> 00:18:10,880
I mean, I the fact that AI and robots are going to be capable of doing more and more society, computing more and more and more and hopefully adding more and more value provided the technology is directed to the right problems is a good thing.

139
00:18:10,960 --> 00:18:13,760
And that leaves me more time to focus on things which really matter in life, right?

140
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:18,560
I mean, like, hopefully, developing my spiritual formation, my human formation, my intellectual formation, right?

141
00:18:18,800 --> 00:18:25,200
Developing better friendships, spending more time with my kids, my wife, you know, learning ways to discern my skills, right?

142
00:18:25,520 --> 00:18:29,760
Like my God given abilities and skills, and trying to figure out how to put those at the service of others.

143
00:18:30,560 --> 00:18:34,880
I think that sometimes when we forget what life's really all, we get focused on the wrong questions.

144
00:18:35,120 --> 00:18:41,040
And that's, I think, in large part, one of the big problems today is where should this incredibly powerful technology be directed?

145
00:18:41,360 --> 00:18:54,000
And I think if we're not keeping it focused on human flourish and we get focused more on getting to Mars or exploring the cosmos, then I think we're moving a lot of really important resources away from the most important problems.

146
00:18:54,960 --> 00:19:07,520
And speaking on evangelization and using this technology for that good, Alessandro, can you share a little bit about how you see us using AI, emerging technology to expand the evangelization efforts of the church?

147
00:19:07,600 --> 00:19:16,000
I mean, the Hello app, of course, is an incredible example of using cell phones, you know, mobile apps to bring prayer and to remind people to pray on a daily basis.

148
00:19:16,160 --> 00:19:16,960
And that's an incredible example.

149
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:22,480
But where else do you see this new technology being able to help us spread the word?

150
00:19:23,520 --> 00:19:25,360
Yeah, I agree with everything Matthew just said.

151
00:19:25,600 --> 00:19:33,840
I think if you view technology as a tool and service of a greater end, as opposed to the end of itself, there's all kinds of creative and hopeful applications.

152
00:19:34,080 --> 00:19:37,520
I went, you asked me a question of hope earlier, and I went to all the reinforcing the negative stuff.

153
00:19:37,600 --> 00:19:39,920
So I don't want to lose track, you know, we are an easter people.

154
00:19:40,640 --> 00:19:50,640
And I think, um, in the same way that we're able to build amazing, uh, cathedrals and paint beautiful art once we had the physical security, you know, establishing, uh, our cities.

155
00:19:51,360 --> 00:19:54,320
and universities, you know, you can progress to higher order of things.

156
00:19:54,880 --> 00:20:11,680
I think one of the great hopes of our tools like, uh, Magisterium AI and AI broadly in the modern world is that it presents a deeply, in some ways, deeply personal and accessible way to access the church's teachings broadly and to engage in spiritual search.

157
00:20:11,840 --> 00:20:23,360
So one of the things we've seen at Halloween is, um, an increasing number of people who are not traditionally associated with the church, whether they're fallen away or not, the spiritual but not religious.

158
00:20:23,920 --> 00:20:38,480
I always think that one of the most optimistic data points is that while there has been a rise of spiritual but not religious people, there are people that identify as such over the last two decades and a decreasing percentage of Catholics, there has not really been a rise of atheism.

159
00:20:39,200 --> 00:20:43,440
And what I would point to is, at least not on that same level.

160
00:20:44,400 --> 00:20:50,160
And I think that speaks to, as a catechism would say, the desire to know and love God is written on the human heart.

161
00:20:50,800 --> 00:21:19,040
